    <h1>Occupational Therapy for Autism at Home: A Parent’s Simple Guide</h1>

    <p>
      Many parents of children with autism wonder if occupational therapy can be done at home.
      The good news is that <strong>occupational therapy for autism at home</strong> can be very effective
      when guided properly by a trained occupational therapist.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <p>
        Home-based occupational therapy helps children develop daily living skills,
        manage sensory challenges, improve attention, and become more independent
        in everyday activities.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Occupational Therapy for Autism?</h2>

    <p>
      Occupational therapy (OT) for children with autism focuses on helping them participate
      in daily activities such as dressing, eating, playing, writing, and social interaction.
    </p>

    <h2>Can Occupational Therapy Be Done at Home?</h2>

    <p>
      Yes. Occupational therapy for autism can be successfully implemented at home,
      especially when parents are guided by an occupational therapist through online sessions.
    </p>

    <h2>Benefits of Occupational Therapy for Autism at Home</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Improves sensory regulation and reduces meltdowns</li>
      <li>Develops fine motor and gross motor skills</li>
      <li>Enhances daily living skills such as dressing and feeding</li>
      <li>Supports attention, focus, and emotional regulation</li>
      <li>Builds independence and confidence</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Simple OT Activities Parents Can Do at Home</h2>

    <h3>Sensory Activities</h3>
    <p>Playing with clay, sand, rice bins, or water play supports sensory processing.</p>

    <h3>Fine Motor Activities</h3>
    <p>Puzzles, drawing, colouring, and bead threading improve hand strength.</p>

    <h3>Gross Motor Activities</h3>
    <p>Jumping, crawling, and obstacle courses improve coordination.</p>

    <h2>When Should Parents Seek Professional Help?</h2>

    <p>
      Professional guidance is recommended if your child struggles with daily routines,
      sensory regulation, or motor development.
    </p>

